# Cloudflare Pages response headers. Parsed by Pages, never served as a file.
# The whole site is first-party (Pico CSS is vendored in /vendor), so the CSP
# can stay this tight; a third-party script (analytics, fonts) needs its origin
# added to the matching directive.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'

# Everything below is safe to pin for a week because the staging step gives the
# URL a content hash (`/styles.css?v=<sha256>`, see scripts/stage-site.sh), so a
# changed file is a new URL rather than a stale one. Note that the zone's
# Browser Cache TTL overrides anything shorter than 4 hours anyway — a `3600`
# here did nothing — which is exactly why the hashes exist.
#
# The splat also catches the hashed form if Pages ever matches on the full URL.
# HTML is deliberately absent: Pages revalidates it on every load, and that is
# what makes the new URLs reach a returning visitor right away.
/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/styles.css*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/app.js*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
